    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Strabo.jpg",
    bio: "Strabo was born in Amaseia, a city in the kingdom of Pontus on the southern coast of the Black Sea, at a moment when Rome was in the process of swallowing the Hellenistic world whole. He was Greek by culture, cosmopolitan by circumstance, and insatiably curious by temperament. Educated in the tradition of the great Stoic and Peripatetic philosophers, he studied under some of the finest scholars of his age — including the geographer Tyrannion and possibly Posidonius of Rhodes, the most influential polymath of the late Hellenistic world. He travelled widely: to Rome, to Egypt (where he sailed the Nile as far as the Ethiopian border), to Asia Minor, Greece, and the western Mediterranean. Few ancient authors had seen as much of the world as they described.\n\nHis great surviving work is the Geographica, a seventeen-volume study of the known world that is the most ambitious geographical treatise to survive from antiquity. Written between roughly 20 BC and AD 7, it was not simply a catalogue of places and distances but a sustained argument about what geography meant as a discipline: its relevance to philosophy, to statecraft, to military strategy, and to the understanding of human cultures. Strabo insisted that the geographer must also be a historian, an ethnographer, and a natural scientist, because no place can be understood in isolation from its people, its past, and the physical forces that shaped it. The Geographica is accordingly enormous in scope — covering Britain to India, Scandinavia to sub-Saharan Africa — and filled with vivid accounts of customs, landscapes, economies, and peoples.\n\nStrabo was not always right. He doubted the spherical calculations of Eratosthenes on some points, mislocated Ireland, and repeated myths alongside facts without always distinguishing between them. But as a synthesiser of ancient geographical knowledge — drawing on Eratosthenes, Polybius, Posidonius, and dozens of others — he was without equal. His description of the Nabataean kingdom and the Arabian incense trade is one of the most detailed accounts to survive from antiquity, written by a man who had spoken to traders who walked the routes himself. Where other ancient geographers gave coordinates, Strabo gave texture: the smell of a harbour, the character of a people, the economics of a trading city.\n\nFor centuries after his death, Strabo\'s Geographica was almost entirely unknown in the Latin West. It survived in Byzantium, consulted by Eastern scholars, and re-entered European awareness only with the recovery of Greek manuscripts in the fifteenth century. Once rediscovered, it became indispensable: the explorers of the Age of Discovery read him eagerly, sometimes finding confirmation of what they had seen, sometimes confronting the vast gap between ancient geography and the actual world. He stands as one of the great connective intellects of antiquity — a man who tried, before the tools existed to do it properly, to describe the entire inhabited Earth in a single, coherent, argumentative whole."
