    summary: "On June 27, 1880, Helen Adams Keller was born in Tuscumbia, Alabama — a healthy child who, before her second birthday, would be struck by an illness (probably scarlet fever or meningitis) that left her both deaf and blind. In an era that offered almost no educational provision for children with combined sensory disabilities, her prognosis was bleak. The conventional wisdom of the time was that such children could not be meaningfully educated, and that the best families could hope for was basic care and supervision. Helen\'s parents, unwilling to accept this verdict, eventually sought help from the Perkins Institution for the Blind in Boston — a decision that would transform not only their daughter\'s life but the world\'s understanding of human potential.\n\nIn 1887, a twenty-year-old teacher named Anne Sullivan arrived at the Keller home and began one of the most remarkable acts of education in recorded history. Working through touch — spelling words into Helen\'s palm, connecting signs to objects with patient, relentless repetition — Sullivan eventually broke through the wall of sensory isolation. The moment Keller understood that the word being spelled into her hand as water poured over it actually meant the thing she was feeling became one of the famous scenes in American autobiography, described in her own memoir with startling clarity and emotion. From that moment, Keller\'s development was explosive. She learned to read Braille, to write, and eventually — astonishingly — to speak. She graduated cum laude from Radcliffe College in 1904, making her the first deaf-blind person to earn a university degree. She went on to become an author, political activist, and international symbol of what human determination and the right education could achieve — a life that began on a June morning in Alabama and ended up rewriting what the world thought was possible.",
